_model: prematter
---
title: A quick trip through the Elements
---
body:

To get an idea of what's in the *Elements,* here are a few highlights in the order that they appear:

#### [Book I](/elements/books/bookI/) on basic plane geometry

- @I.Def.23, definition of parallel lines, one of many definitions in [Book I](/elements/books/bookI/#defs)
- @I.Post.5, the parallel postulate
- [Common notions](/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/), the axioms for magnitudes
- @I.1, the first proposition which shows how to construct an equilateral triangle
- The congruence theorems for triangles: @I.4, side-angle-side, @I.8, side-side-side, and @I.26, angle-side-angle
- Propositions on isosceles triangles: @I.5, equal angles imply equal sides, and the converse, @I.6, equal sides imply equal angles
- @I.9 and @I.10, constructions to bisect angles and line segments
- @I.11 and @I.12, constructions to draw perpendicular lines
- @I.16, an exterior angle of a triangle is greater than either of the opposite interior angles (compare I.32 below)
- @I.29, about angles made when a line crosses two parallel lines
- @I.20, the triangle inequality (the sum of two sides is greater than the third)
- @I.22, to construct a triangle with given sides
- @I.32, an exterior angle of a triangle is the sum of the two opposite interior angles; the sum of the three interior angles equals two right angles.
- On application of areas: @I.42 to find a parallelogram equal in area to any given triangle, and @I.45 to find a parallelogram equal in area to any given polygon
- @I.47, the Pythagorean theorem and its converse @I.48

#### [Book II](/elements/books/bookII/) on geometric algebra

- @II.4, a geometric version of the algebraic identity (*x* + *y*)² = *x*² + 2*xy* + *y*²
- @II.5, a sample proposition showing how to factor the difference of two squares
- @II.6, a geometric version to solve the quadratic equation (*b* – *x*)*x* = *c*
- @II.11, construction to cut a line in the golden ratio
- @II.12 and @II.13, a pre-trigonometry version of the law of cosines
- @II.14, a final proposition on application of areas—to find a square equal in area to any given polygon

#### [Book III](/elements/books/bookIII/) on circles and angles

- @III.1, how to find the center of a circle
- @III.17, how to draw a line tangent to a circle
- Propositions on angles in circles: @III.20, @III.21, and @III.22
- @III.31, Thales' theorem that an angle inscribed in a semicircle is right, and similar statements giving acute and obtuse angles
- @III.35, when two chords are drawn through a point inside a circle, then the product of the two segments of one chord equals the product of the two segments of the other chord
- @III.36, if from a point outside a circle both a tangent and a secant are drawn, then the square of the tangent is the product of the whole secant and the external segment of the secant, and the converse in @III.37

#### [Book IV](/elements/books/bookIV/) on constructions of regular polygons

- Inscribing and circumscribing circles and arbitrary triangles @IV.2, @IV.3, @IV.4, and @IV.5,
- @IV.10, how to construct a particular triangle needed for regular pentagons
- @IV.11, how to construct a regular pentagon
- @IV.15, how to construct a regular hexagon
- @IV.16, how to construct a regular 15-gon

#### [Book V](/elements/books/bookV/) on Eudoxus' abstract theory of ratio and proportion, abstract algebra

- @V.Def.3, the definition and nature of ratio
- @V.Def.5 and V.6, the definition of proportion (equality of ratios)
- @V.Def.9, the definition of duplicate proportion (the square of a ratio)
- @V.2, distributivity of multiplication over addition
- @V.3, associativity of multiplication of whole numbers
- @V.11, transitivity of equality of ratios
- @V.16, alternate proportions
- @V.22, ratios *ex aequali*

#### [Book VI](/elements/books/bookVI/) on similar figures and geometric proportions

- @VI.Def.1, definition of similar figures
- @VI.1, areas of triangles (also parallelograms) of the same height are proportional to their bases
- @VI.2, a line parallel to the base of a triangle cuts the sides proportionally
- Propositions on similar triangles: @VI.4, @VI.5,
- @VI.6, side-angle-side similarity theorem
- @VI.9, to cut a line into a given number of equal segments
- @VI.10, to cut a line into a specified ratio
- Constructions of fourth proportionals @VI.12, and mean proportionals @VI.13,
- @VI.16, if four lines are proportional, *w* : *x* = *y*:*z*, then the rectangle contained by the extremes, *w* by *z*, has the same area as the rectangle contained by the means, *x* by *y*
- @VI.19, on areas of similar triangles
- @VI.25, on application of areas
- @VI.31, a generalization of the Pythagorean theorem to figures other than squares

#### [Book VII](/elements/books/bookVII/) on basic number theory

- @VII.Def.11, definition of prime number
- @VII.12, the Euclidean algorithm for finding greatest common divisors
- Several basic properties of numbers, such as @VII.16, commutativity of multiplication of numbers, *mn* = *nm.*
- @VII.29, if a prime number doesn't divide a number, then it is relatively prime to it
- @VII.30, if a prime number divides a product of two numbers, then it divides one of them
- @VII.34, on constructing least common multiples

#### [Book VIII](/elements/books/bookVIII/) on continued proportions (geometric progressions) in number theory

- @VIII.2 and @VIII.4, on finding continued proportions of numbers
- Many propositions on squares and cubes, such as @VIII.22, if three numbers are in continued proportion, and the first is square, then the third is also square

#### [Book IX](/elements/books/bookIX/) on number theory

- @IX.14, a partial version of the fundamental theorem of arithmetic that says no prime number can divide a product of other prime numbers
- @IX.20, there are infinitely many prime numbers
- Several propositions on even and odd numbers, such as @IX.23 which says that if you add an odd number of odd numbers together, then the sum is odd
- @IX.35, how to get the sum of a geometric progression
- @IX.36, on perfect numbers

#### [Book X](/elements/books/bookX/) on classification of irrational magnitudes

- @X.Def.I.1, definition of commensurable magnitudes
- @X.1, a principle of exhaustion
- @X.2, a characterization of incommensurable magnitudes
- @X.9, commensurability in square as opposed to commensurability in length
- @X.12, transitivity of commensurability
- @X.26, the difference between two medial areas is irrational
- [Lemma 1](/elements/books/bookX/propX29/#lemma1) for Prop. X.29, to find two square numbers whose sum is also a square

#### [Book XI](/elements/books/bookXI/) on basic solid geometry

- @XI.Def.14, definition of a sphere
- @XI.Def.25 through 28, definitions of regular polygons
- @XI.3, the intersection of two planes is a straight line
- @XI.6, two lines perpendicular to a plane are parallel
- Constructions to draw lines perpendicular to planes: @XI.11 and @XI.12
- @XI.14, two planes perpendicular to the same line are parallel
- @XI.23, how to construct solid angles
- Several propositions on volumes of parallelopipeds, such as @XI.32
- @XI.39 on volumes of prisms

#### [Book XII](/elements/books/bookXII/) on measurement of solids

- @XII.2, areas of circle are proportional to the squares on their diameters
- @XII.6 and @XII.7, a triangular prism can be divided into three pyramids of equal volume, hence, the volume of a pyramid is one third of that of the prism with the same base and same height
- @XII.10, the volume of a cone is one third of that of the cylindar with the same base and same height
- @XII.11, volumes of cones and cylinders are proportional to their heights
- @XII.18, on volumes of spheres

#### [Book XIII](/elements/books/bookXIII/) on constructing regular polyhedra

- @XIII.9, on hexagons and decagons inscribed in a circle, and the golden ratio
- @XIII.10, on hexagons and decagons inscribed in a circle, and the golden ratio
- @XIII.11, when a pentagon, hexagon, and decagon are inscribed in a circle, the square on the side of the pentagon equals the sum of the squares on the sides of the hexagon and the decagon
- Constructions of regular polyhedra @XIII.13, @XIII.15, @XIII.14, @XIII.16, and @XIII.17
- These five are shown to be the only regular solids in proposition @XIII.18.
